- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs? 
- What are potential pitfalls with compiler optimizations on such patterns?

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to derive complex bit-pattern metrics with constant-time bit tricks, not brute force.

## Key Concepts

- Identify run starts with n & ~(n << 1)
- Implement a portable popcount64 using parallel masks
- Edge-case handling for 0 and large runs

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

static inline unsigned countRuns64(uint64_t n) {
  return popcount64(n & ~(n << 1));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to an 128-bit value?
- What are the performance implications on large-scale bitmap indexes in practice?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) bit counting and handling of 128-bit data without branching.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR-based popcount
- 128-bit data split into 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling tricks

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned pop64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (unsigned)(v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize with SIMD intrinsics on x86 or ARM?
- How would you unit-test edge cases: all zeros, all ones, randoms?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests advanced bit manipulation skills, requiring multi-word arithmetic without 128-bit native support. It demonstrates branchless programming techniques and careful handling of shift operations.

## Key Concepts
- Represent 128-bit values as two 64-bit words (hi, lo)
- Decompose k into t = k mod 64 (intra-word shift) and b = k div 64 (cross-word flag)
- Branchless selection using bitwise operations and masks
- Handle edge cases like k=0 and k=64 without conditionals

## Code Example
```c
void rotate_left_128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned k, 
                    uint64_t* out_hi, uint64_t* out_lo) {
    uint64_t t = k & 63;
    uint64_t b = (k >> 6) & 1;

    uint64_t hiA = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loA = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t hiB = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loB = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));

    *out_hi = hiA ^ ((hiA ^ hiB) & -b);
    *out_lo = loA ^ ((loA ^ loB) & -b);
}
```

## Examples
- k=1, hi=0, lo=1 → out_hi=1, out_lo=0
- k=64, hi=1, lo=0 → out_hi=0, out_lo=1

## Why This Is Asked
Tests compact, branch-free parity calculation using classic bit-twiddling techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Parity calculation through XOR folding
- Bitwise shifts and XOR operations
- Elimination of loops and conditional branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function parity32(n) {
  n ^= n >>> 16;
  n ^= n >>> 8;
  n ^= n >>> 4;
  n ^= n >>> 2;
  n ^= n >>> 1;
  return n & 1;
}
```

